--- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.6$ | $0.1$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{116}$ be a random sample of size $n=116$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is between $3$ and $5.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484225 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5574158c-e378-5c22-867d-7f3ecb6e0b26" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484225 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=116,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.6+5\\cdot 0.1+10\\cdot 0.2+15\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =0+0.5+2+1.5 \\\\  & =4,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =({0}^{2}\\cdot 0.6+{5}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{10}^{2}\\cdot 0.2+{15}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{4}^{2} \\\\  & =(0+2.5+20+22.5)-16 \\\\  & =29.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(4,\\frac{29}{116}) \\\\  & ∼N(4,0.25).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5) & ≈P(\\frac{3-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}<Z<\\frac{5-4}{\\sqrt{0.25}}) \\\\  & =P(-2<Z<2) \\\\  & =Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(3<\\overset{―}{X}<5)=Φ(2)-Φ(-2).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484332 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186484351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484332 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e72641b-14ce-5981-bb0d-51d727a8f321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484332 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064506 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484336 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186484352 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484336 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b6a43a1-9e1f-512b-801a-9425c6c455d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484336 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186484259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484345 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ba1b50f8-7d5b-583a-ba63-4a95a4627651" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484345 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186484262 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484351 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186484345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484351 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ca0175c6-365a-5159-8b93-9102cbcb7847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484351 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186484225 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484352 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186484332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484352 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d0097ec8-75db-516a-b1e4-c90a66598b83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484352 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186484253 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
